ality prevalence = number abnormal frogs/total frogs sampled at all sites over all years.</p></fn><fn id="tfn3-ehp0116-001009"><label>b</label><p>Number of breeding sites sampled.</p></fn><fn id="tfn4-ehp0116-001009"><label>c</label><p>For the median and range of breeding site abnormality prevalence we did not calculate prevalence of skeletal and eye abnormalities for ponds at which &#x0003c; 50 individuals were examined.</p></fn></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap><table-wrap id="t2-ehp0116-001009" position="float"><label>Table 2</label><caption><p>Summary of abnormalities in wood frog populations at five national wildlife refuges in Alaska.</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><thead><tr><th align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><th colspan="6" align="center" rowspan="1">No. of abnormalities<hr/></th></tr><tr><th align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Abnormality</th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Arctic</th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Innoko</th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Kenai</th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Tetlin</th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Yukon Delta</th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Total</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td colspan="7" align="left" rowspan="1">Eye abnormality</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;Anophthalmia (missing eye)</td><td align="right" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0</td><td align="right" rowspan="1" colspan="1">2</td><td align="right" rowspan="1" colspan="1">12</td><td align="right" rowspan="1" colspan="1">6</td><td align="right" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0</td><td align="right" rowspan="1" colspan="1">20</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;Unpigmented iris (black eye)</td><td align="right" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0</td><td align="right" rowspan="1" colspan="1">15</td><td align="right" rowspan="1" colspan="1">118</td><td align="right" rowspan="1" colspan="1">20</td><td align="right" rowspan="1" co